testinal toxicity","labels":"Chemical,Disease","metrics":{"precision":0.25,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.4,"tp":3,"fp":9,"fn":0,"rel_precision":0.25,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":0.4,"rel_tp":3,"rel_fp":9,"rel_fn":0},"pairs":[{"head":"cis-platinum","tail":"ovarian cancer","head_id":"1","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"cis-platinum","tail":"anemia","head_id":"1","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"cis-platinum","tail":"gastrointestinal toxicity","head_id":"1","tail_id":"3"},{"head":"cis-platinum","tail":"nephrotoxicity","head_id":"1","tail_id":"4"},{"head":"adriamycin","tail":"ovarian cancer","head_id":"2","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"adriamycin","tail":"anemia","head_id":"2","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"adriamycin","tail":"gastrointestinal toxicity","head_id":"2","tail_id":"3"},{"head":"adriamycin","tail":"nephrotoxicity","head_id":"2","tail_id":"4"},{"head":"cyclophosphamide","tail":"ovarian cancer","head_id":"3","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"cyclophosphamide","tail":"anemia","head_id":"3","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"cyclophosphamide","tail":"gastrointestinal toxicity","head_id":"3","tail_id":"3"},{"head":"cyclophosphamide","tail":"nephrotoxicity","head_id":"3","tail_id":"4"},{"head":"hexamethylmelamine","tail":"ovarian cancer","head_id":"4","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"hexamethylmelamine","tail":"anemia","head_id":"4","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"hexamethylmelamine","tail":"gastrointestinal toxicity","head_id":"4","tail_id":"3"},{"head":"hexamethylmelamine","tail":"nephrotoxicity","head_id":"4","tail_id":"4"}],"relations":["1 | 2","1 | 3","1 | 4","2 | 2","2 | 3","2 | 4","3 | 2","3 | 3","3 | 4","4 | 2","4 | 3","4 | 4"],"text":"Treatment of ovarian cancer with a combination of cis-platinum, adriamycin, cyclophosphamide and hexamethylmelamine. During the last 2 1/2 years, 38 patients with ovarian cancer were treated with a combination of cisplatinum (CPDD), 50 mg/m2, adriamycin, 30 mg/m2, cyclophosphamide, 300 mg/m2, on day 1; and hexamethylmelamine (HMM), 6 mg/kg daily, for 14 days. Each course was repeated monthly. 2 patients had stage II, 14 stage III and 22 stage IV disease. 14 of the 38 patients were previously treated with chemotherapy, 1 with radiation, 6 with both chemotherapy and radiation, and 17 did not have any treatment before CPDD combination. 31 of the 38 cases (81.5%) demonstrated objective responses lasting for 2 months or more. These responses were partial in 19 and complete in 12 cases. Hematologic toxicity was moderate and with reversible anemia developing in 71% of patients. Gastrointestinal side effects from CPDD were universal. HMM gastrointestinal toxicity necessitated discontinuation of the drug in 5 patients. Severe nephrotoxicity was observed in 2 patients but was reversible. There were no drug-related deaths."}